INT. GLASSBLOWER'S FURNACE HALL, MURANO - NIGHT

Venice, 1740. A low arched hall lit by the red mouth of a great furnace. Racks of blowpipes. Shelves of unfinished goblets glinting like frozen water. The air shimmers with heat.

MAESTRO ZUANNE BAROVIER-ADEYEMI (50s, leather apron, burned forearms) lifts a glowing gather of glass from the furnace on the end of a pipe. Across the hall, his daughter SERENELLA BAROVIER (20, hair bound in a kerchief, steady hands) stands over a marver with a pair of jacks. Between them, trembling in the heat, stands a young NOBLEMAN: ALVISE CONTARINI-DIAZ (25, velvet coat, silk stockings, wig askew), who has no business here at this hour.

Zuanne turns the pipe. Serenella shapes the neck of a half-formed vase. Alvise steps closer; she shifts her stance between him and her father without looking up.

ZUANNE
You are in my light, Signor.

ALVISE
I beg your pardon, Maestro.

ZUANNE
Move.

ALVISE
I wish to speak to your daughter.

ZUANNE
She is working.

ALVISE
I will wait.

Serenella lowers her jacks. Zuanne lowers the pipe. They look at each other across the hall, and then, as one, they look at Alvise.

SERENELLA
(together)
Signor, it is not wise.

ZUANNE ^
(together)
Signor, it is not wise.

Alvise swallows. Then, with great ceremony, he goes down on one knee in the glassworks sawdust. The furnace roars.

ALVISE
Maestro. I ask for your daughter's hand.

Zuanne's gather falls from the pipe and shatters on the stone in a spray of molten glass. Serenella drops the jacks. Alvise throws an arm over his face. They all scramble back as the glass pools and cools and goes opaque.

Silence. A long one.

ZUANNE
(low)
You proposed in a glassworks.

ALVISE
It seemed the honest place.

SERENELLA
You could have asked in the church.

ALVISE
I asked in the church. Your father's apprentice said you were here.

SERENELLA
I am always here.

ALVISE
I know. It is why I love you.

Serenella looks at her father. Zuanne looks at the floor, at the dark ruin of his gather. His mouth works. Then he picks up a new pipe and walks to the furnace and thrusts it in.

ZUANNE
Hold the door.

ALVISE
Maestro?

ZUANNE
If you wish to marry my daughter, you will learn to hold the door.

Alvise scrambles up. Serenella almost smiles. He takes the iron handle of the furnace door, and the heat strikes his face, and he does not let go.
